                        SECTION 1: INTRODUCTION TO STACK
===============================================================================

🔹 WHAT IS A STACK?
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

A Stack is a LINEAR DATA STRUCTURE that follows a particular order in which
operations are performed. The order is LIFO (Last In First Out) or FILO 
(First In Last Out).

Think of it like:
📚 A stack of plates - You add plates on top and remove from top
📖 A pile of books - Last book placed is the first one picked up
🥞 Stack of pancakes - You eat the top pancake first

🔹 KEY CHARACTERISTICS:
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

✅ LIFO Principle: Last element added is the first one removed
✅ Single Access Point: Elements can only be added/removed from TOP
✅ Dynamic Size: Can grow and shrink as needed (with limitations in arrays)
✅ Fast Operations: All operations are O(1) time complexity

🔹 REAL-WORLD APPLICATIONS:
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

1. Function Call Stack (Recursion)
   - Every function call is pushed onto the call stack
   - When function returns, it's popped from the stack

2. Undo/Redo Operations
   - Text editors use stacks for undo (Ctrl+Z) and redo (Ctrl+Y)
   - Each action is pushed onto undo stack

3. Browser History
   - Back button uses stack to navigate previous pages
   - Forward button uses another stack

4. Expression Evaluation
   - Converting infix to postfix/prefix
   - Evaluating mathematical expressions

5. Syntax Parsing
   - Checking balanced parentheses in code
   - Compiler syntax checking

6. Backtracking Algorithms
   - Maze solving
   - DFS in graphs/trees

7. Memory Management
   - Stack memory allocation for local variables

